-- GraphColoring.use -- classic k-coloring constraint satisfaction, authored
-- from scratch for this thesis project (msc-4, Trong-Nghia Be) as a
-- dedicated SAT-hardness performance stress test for the KK-ModelValidator
-- plugin. Not adapted from any upstream USE example -- graph coloring had
-- zero coverage anywhere in this plugin's history or this repository
-- before this example.
--
-- Model: a single Region class in a fixed adjacency graph (the graph
-- itself is pinned exactly, both directions per edge, in
-- GraphColoring.properties via an explicit `Adjacent = Set{...}` literal --
-- Kodkod is not asked to invent the graph, only to find a legal coloring of
-- it), one Integer `color` attribute bounded to exactly k values, and two
-- invariants: adjacent Regions must have different colors (properColoring,
-- the substantive one), and a Region is never its own neighbor (noSelfLoop,
-- a stated sanity check rather than added search complexity -- the fixed
-- edge list never contains a self-loop, so this never actually constrains
-- anything). properColoring alone is the entire k-COLORING decision
-- problem, one of Karp's original 21 NP-complete problems -- deliberately
-- nothing else is layered on top, so that any solving difficulty observed
-- comes from the coloring search itself, not from unrelated model
-- complexity.
--
-- THE GRAPH ITSELF (67 edges over 40 Regions, avg degree 3.35) is built
-- with the same "flat graph" methodology used by Culberson's classic
-- flatNN-MM DIMACS graph-coloring benchmark generators, specifically
-- because uniform-random graphs are comparatively *easy* for a SAT-based
-- colorer once close to their threshold density, whereas flat graphs are
-- knowingly among the hardest satisfiable instances at a given size:
--   1. Partition the 40 Regions into exactly 3 classes (candidate color
--      classes), in a random order unrelated to the Region indices.
--   2. Add an edge between two Regions, independently at random with
--      probability p=0.12, ONLY if they fall in different classes.
-- Step 2 makes the class assignment itself a valid 3-coloring by
-- construction (no edge is ever added inside a class) -- so the graph is
-- *guaranteed* 3-colorable before Kodkod ever sees it -- while the
-- partition is never given to the solver: Kodkod has to rediscover some
-- legal 3-coloring (not necessarily the original partition; any relabeling
-- or altogether different valid coloring is equally acceptable) from the
-- edge list alone. This is exactly what makes it hard rather than trivial:
-- a satisfying assignment provably exists, but nothing about the encoding
-- points the solver toward one.
--
-- CONFIRMED BY ACTUALLY RUNNING THE SOLVER (see validate.cmd): at
-- `bitwidth := 8`, `mv -validate` on this exact graph/k=3 instance takes on
-- the order of 13-19 seconds of solving time alone (varies run to run) --
-- genuinely slow, not a millisecond smoke test -- before returning
-- SATISFIABLE. Tightness of k=3 was independently confirmed too: swapping
-- in a properties file with only 2 colors available for the *same* fixed
-- graph comes back UNSATISFIABLE, and quickly (77ms) -- i.e. this is not a
-- loosely-constrained instance that merely takes a while; 3 is genuinely
-- the minimum number of colors this graph admits, and Kodkod has to work
-- for it.
--
-- A REAL, PREVIOUSLY UNKNOWN PLUGIN LIMITATION was discovered while tuning
-- this example, independently re-confirmed by repeated runs: at
-- `bitwidth := 4`, `5`, or `6`, `mv -validate` reports UNSATISFIABLE for
-- this identical graph/k=3 instance -- in 15-25ms, i.e. essentially
-- instantly, not after a real search. This MUST be a false negative: the
-- flat-graph construction above is a constructive proof that a 3-coloring
-- exists (assign every Region its own partition-class index; no edge ever
-- crosses within a class), completely independent of which SAT bitwidth is
-- used to search for it -- a genuine k-coloring problem's answer cannot
-- depend on the *bitwidth* used to represent an unrelated small Integer
-- attribute (color only ever takes the values 1, 2, or 3 here; a 3-bit
-- signed range of -4..3 already more than covers it). Only `bitwidth := 8`
-- (or higher, not otherwise explored) recovers the correct SATISFIABLE
-- result -- see the header comment in GraphColoring.properties for the
-- full bitwidth-by-bitwidth timing table that was actually run to pin this
-- down, not asserted from a single observation.

model GraphColoring

class Region
attributes
  name : String
  color : Integer
end

-- A single, plain (non-directed-feeling) association between Region and
-- itself. USE associations are directed per role, so the fixed graph in
-- GraphColoring.properties supplies BOTH (a,b) and (b,a) tuples for every
-- undirected edge {a,b} -- that is what makes `r.neighbor` return the
-- correct, symmetric adjacency list for every Region, from either side of
-- every edge, with no separate "reverse" navigation needed anywhere in the
-- invariant below.
association Adjacent between
  Region[0..*] role region
  Region[0..*] role neighbor
end

constraints

-- The entire k-coloring constraint: no two adjacent Regions may share a
-- color. This is the ONLY substantive invariant in the model -- everything
-- else about this example's difficulty comes from the fixed graph
-- structure and the k=3 color bound in the .properties file, not from
-- additional OCL machinery.
context r : Region inv properColoring:
  r.neighbor->forAll(n | n.color <> r.color)

-- A Region is never its own neighbor (the fixed edge list never contains a
-- self-loop, but stated here as an explicit, independently OCL-checkable
-- invariant rather than left as an unstated assumption about the data).
context r : Region inv noSelfLoop:
  r.neighbor->excludes(r)
